"3-Cordial Colorings (https://dwest.web.illinois.edu/regs/3cordial.html)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 35.9 years ago; Crawled: 16.4 years ago; Conjecture 1 (Hovey): All connected graphs are 3-cordial. ... A graph G is cordial if and only if it is 2-cordial; Cahit [C] proved that all trees are cordial.\n# 3-Cordial Colorings (1991)\n\nOriginator: M. Hovey    (presented by Bill Kinnersley - REGS 2010)\n\nDefinitions: To each 3-coloring f of a graph G, associate a 3-edge-coloring f' of G as follows. For each edge uv in G,\n\n  * if u and v have the same color under f, then give uv that color under f'.\n  * if u and v have different colors under f, then give uv the third color under f'.\nWe call f a 3-cordial coloring of G if the sizes of color classes of vertices under f differ by at most 1 and the sizes of color classes of edges under f' also differ by at most 1. If G has a 3-cordial coloring, then we say that G is 3-cordial. Note that a 3-cordial coloring need not be a proper coloring.\n\nBackground: 3-cordial colorings were introduced by Hovey [H] (though the formulation above is due to Edwards [E]). Hovey showed that all paths, cycles, complete graphs, complete bipartite graphs, and forests are 3-cordial.\n\nConjecture 1 (Hovey): All connected graphs are 3-cordial.\n\nComments: The connectedness requirement in Conjecture 1 may be unnecessary. Hovey unified these conjectures and result as special cases of a more general conjecture.\n\nConjecture 2 (Hovey [H]): Every tree is k-cordial for every k.\n\nComments: Hovey [H] proved Conjecture 3 for k∈{3,4,5}; as noted above, Cahit [C] had proved it for k=2. Hovey proved that every caterpillar is k-cordial for every k. He also gave a (necessarily terminating) algorithm to check whether all trees are k-cordial for any fixed k. This algorithm involves heavy case analysis and gets quite tedious as k gets large, but could probably be executed with computer assistance for the next few values of k.\n\nHovey noted that when k ≠ 3, not all graphs are k-cordial: C_{6} is not 2-cordial, and K_{2,k-2} is not k-cordial for k ≥ 4. He also proved that sufficiently dense graphs are not k-cordial whenever k is even and at least 6. Tao [T] studied the property for other special graphs. Youssef [Y] showed that K_{n} is 4-cordial if and only if n≤6 and that K_{r,s} is 4-cordial if r and s are not both odd multiples of 2.\n\nNon-cyclic groups may also be of interest; the first to consider is F_{2}×F_{2}. In REGS 2010, Pechenik and Wise have proved that a path with n vertices is F_{2}×F_{2}-cordial if and only if n∉{4,5}.\n\nQuestion 3: Which graphs (or which trees) are F_{2}×F_{2}-cordial?\n\nReferences:\n[C] I. Cahit, Cordial graphs: a weaker version of graceful and harmonious graphs, Ars Combin. 23 (1987), 201--207.\n[CHR] G. Chang, D. Hsu, and D. Rogers, Additive variations on a graceful theme, Utilitas Math. 32 (1987), 181--197.\n[E] K. Edwards, Research problems from the BCC21, compiled by P.A. Cameron, Discrete Math. 310 (2010), 347--354.\n[GS] R.L. Graham and N.J.A. Sloane, On additive bases and harmonious graphs, SIAM J. Algebraic Discrete Methods 1 (1980) 382--404.\n[H] M. Hovey, A-cordial graphs, Discrete Math. 93 (1991) 183--194.\n[T] R. Tao, On k-cordiality of cycles, crowns, and wheels, System Sci. Math. Sci. 11 (1998), 227-229.\n[Y] M.Z. Youssef, On k-cordial labelling, Australasian J. Combin. 43 (2009), 31--37.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nk < j < 2k; C\\_{(2m+1)k} is not k-cordial; K\\_m is 3-cordial; and, for k even, K\\_{mk} is k-cordial if and only if m = 1. (https://www.combinatorics.org/files/Surveys/ds6/ds6v26-2023.pdf)\nciteturn2search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.9 years ago; Hovey advances the following conjectures: all trees are k-cordial for all k; all connected graphs are 3-cordial; and C\\_{2mk+j} is k-cordial if and only if j ≠ k, where k and j are even and 0 ≤ j < 2k. ... Erickson [895] et al. showed